# 19. BASIC in programming-language history

Considering the languages you've been asking about, we can place BASIC into a broader historical picture:

```text
1950s
  │
  ├── FORTRAN ─── scientific/numerical computing
  │
1960s
  │
  ├── ALGOL ───── structured programming
  │
  ├── COBOL ───── business/data processing
  │
  ├── Lisp ────── symbolic/AI programming
  │
  └── BASIC ───── beginner/educational programming
          │
1970s
  │
  ├── C ───────── systems programming
  │
  └── Smalltalk ─ object-oriented programming
```

This gives you a useful conceptual map:

| Language      | Major historical role            |
| ------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **FORTRAN**   | Scientific computing             |
| **COBOL**     | Business/data processing         |
| **ALGOL**     | Structured programming           |
| **Lisp**      | Symbolic computation / AI        |
| **BASIC**     | Beginner & personal computing    |
| **C**         | Systems programming              |
| **Smalltalk** | Pure object-oriented programming |

