# 20. The big picture

One way to understand the evolution is:

```text
Machine
   │
   ▼
Assembly
   │
   ▼
FORTRAN ─────── make numerical programming easier
   │
ALGOL ───────── make program structure clearer
   │
COBOL ───────── make business programming easier
   │
Lisp ────────── make symbolic computation powerful
   │
BASIC ───────── make programming accessible
   │
C ───────────── make systems programming practical
   │
Smalltalk ───── make object-oriented programming pure
   │
   ▼
Modern languages
```

So **BASIC's historical contribution was not primarily technical low-level power**. Its major contribution was cultural and educational:

> **It helped turn programming from a specialized activity into something ordinary students and personal-computer users could experiment with themselves.**

For your programming-language history study, I would put **BASIC alongside FORTRAN, COBOL, ALGOL, Lisp, C, and Smalltalk** because each represents a different important direction in the development of programming languages.
